### What is Stream.generate?

`Stream.generate` creates a stream whose elements all come from a single supplier. For each element it calls `supplier.get()` and uses the result. The supplier takes no input and does not see the previous element, so every element is produced independently.

```java
package com.bank.payment;

import java.util.stream.Stream;

public class GenerateBasics {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Supplier returns a constant; generate repeats it.
        Stream.generate(() -> "PING")
                .limit(3)
                .forEach(System.out::println);
    }
}
```

```text
PING
PING
PING
```

### How do you generate unique transaction reference numbers?

Use a supplier that returns a fresh unique value each call, such as a UUID-based reference. `limit(n)` fixes the batch size.

```java
package com.bank.payment;

import java.util.List;
import java.util.UUID;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;
import java.util.stream.Stream;

public class ReferenceGenerator {
    public List<String> generateReferences(int count) {
        return Stream.generate(() -> "TXN-" + UUID.randomUUID())
                .limit(count)
                .collect(Collectors.toList());
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        new ReferenceGenerator().generateReferences(5)
                .forEach(System.out::println);
    }
}
```

```text
TXN-5f2b1c9a-7d33-4e21-9b0a-1c2d3e4f5a6b
TXN-a1b2c3d4-e5f6-4789-90ab-cdef01234567
TXN-9988aa77-6655-4433-2211-00ffeeddccbb
TXN-13579bdf-2468-4ace-8135-79bdf2468ace
TXN-0a1b2c3d-4e5f-4061-8273-849506172839
```

Each UUID is unique, so every reference in the batch is distinct.

### Why is Stream.generate infinite?

There is no stopping condition in `generate`. The supplier can be called an unlimited number of times, so the stream describes an endless source. You decide how much of it to consume with `limit`.

```java
// WRONG: no bound, the supplier is called forever
Stream.generate(() -> "TXN-" + java.util.UUID.randomUUID())
        .forEach(System.out::println); // never terminates
```

```java
// CORRECT: limit(5) calls the supplier five times, then stops
Stream.generate(() -> "TXN-" + java.util.UUID.randomUUID())
        .limit(5)
        .forEach(System.out::println);
```

The wrong version prints references until you kill the process. The correct version stops at five.

### How does limit bound the infinite stream?

`limit(n)` is a short-circuiting intermediate operation. Because the stream is lazy, the supplier is called only when `limit` requests another element, and `limit` requests exactly `n` of them.

```java
package com.bank.payment;

import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger;
import java.util.stream.Stream;

public class SupplierCallCount {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        AtomicInteger calls = new AtomicInteger();
        Stream.generate(() -> {
                    calls.incrementAndGet();
                    return "TXN";
                })
                .limit(4)
                .forEach(v -> {});
        System.out.println("supplier called " + calls.get() + " times");
    }
}
```

```text
supplier called 4 times
```

The supplier runs exactly four times, proving `limit` bounds the lazy source precisely.

### How does Stream.generate differ from Stream.iterate?

This is the core comparison. `generate` calls a supplier that ignores the previous value, so each element is independent. `iterate` applies a function to the previous value, so each element depends on the one before it.

| Aspect | Stream.generate | Stream.iterate |
|--------|------------------|-----------------|
| Source | `Supplier<T>` | seed + `UnaryOperator<T>` |
| Depends on previous | no | yes |
| Has a seed | no | yes |
| Ordered | unordered | ordered |
| Parallelizes | yes, elements independent | poorly, dependent chain |
| Typical use | random IDs, constants, sensor reads | dates, balances, counters |
| Bounding | `limit` | `limit` or 3-arg predicate |

```java
package com.bank.payment;

import java.util.concurrent.ThreadLocalRandom;
import java.util.stream.Stream;

public class GenerateVsIterate {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("generate (independent):");
        Stream.generate(() -> ThreadLocalRandom.current().nextInt(1000))
                .limit(3)
                .forEach(System.out::println);

        System.out.println("iterate (dependent):");
        Stream.iterate(1000, n -> n + 1)
                .limit(3)
                .forEach(System.out::println);
    }
}
```

```text
generate (independent):
527
83
914
iterate (dependent):
1000
1001
1002
```

### What are common mistakes with Stream.generate?

The most common mistake is omitting `limit`, which hangs. A subtler mistake is relying on generate to produce a counting or dependent sequence, which it cannot do because the supplier has no memory of the previous value.

```java
// WRONG: trying to count with generate; the supplier cannot see the last value
int[] n = {0};
Stream.generate(() -> n[0]++)   // works only via an external mutable hack
        .limit(3)
        .forEach(System.out::println);
```

```java
// CORRECT: for a counting sequence use iterate, which is built for it
Stream.iterate(0, i -> i + 1)
        .limit(3)
        .forEach(System.out::println);
```

The wrong version leans on a shared mutable array, which breaks under parallelism. For a dependent sequence, `iterate` is the right tool.

### How do you guarantee uniqueness across a batch?

UUIDs are practically unique, but if you need a readable sequence you can combine generate with an atomic counter wrapped in the supplier, or better, use a thread-safe source. Here is a readable reference with a timestamp prefix and a counter.

```java
package com.bank.payment;

import java.util.List;
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicLong;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;
import java.util.stream.Stream;

public class ReadableReferences {
    public List<String> batch(int count) {
        AtomicLong seq = new AtomicLong(1);
        String day = "20240115";
        return Stream.generate(() -> "TXN-" + day + "-" + seq.getAndIncrement())
                .limit(count)
                .collect(Collectors.toList());
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        new ReadableReferences().batch(4).forEach(System.out::println);
    }
}
```

```text
TXN-20240115-1
TXN-20240115-2
TXN-20240115-3
TXN-20240115-4
```

The `AtomicLong` keeps the counter thread-safe even if the stream is parallelized.

### What are the edge cases?

A `limit(0)` yields an empty stream and never calls the supplier. A supplier that returns `null` produces a stream of nulls, which usually signals a bug downstream.

```java
package com.bank.payment;

import java.util.stream.Stream;

public class EdgeCases {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        long zero = Stream.generate(() -> "TXN").limit(0).count();
        System.out.println("limit(0) count = " + zero);

        // A null-returning supplier is legal but risky.
        Stream.generate(() -> (String) null).limit(2)
                .forEach(v -> System.out.println("value = " + v));
    }
}
```

```text
limit(0) count = 0
value = null
value = null
```

### What about thread safety and parallelism?

Because elements are independent, `generate` can run in parallel, but the supplier itself must be thread-safe. A plain `Random` shared across threads contends, so prefer `ThreadLocalRandom` or an atomic counter.

```java
package com.bank.payment;

import java.util.UUID;
import java.util.stream.Stream;

public class ParallelGenerate {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // UUID.randomUUID() is thread-safe, so parallel is fine here.
        long distinct = Stream.generate(() -> UUID.randomUUID().toString())
                .limit(10_000)
                .parallel()
                .distinct()
                .count();
        System.out.println("distinct references = " + distinct);
    }
}
```

```text
distinct references = 10000
```

### When should you use Stream.generate?

Use `generate` when each element is independent of the others: unique IDs, random samples, constant fill values, mock test data, or repeated sensor reads. Use `iterate` instead when each element is computed from the previous one.

```java
package com.bank.payment;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;
import java.util.stream.Stream;

public class MockData {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Fill a batch of pending payments with a constant placeholder amount.
        List<BigDecimal> pending = Stream.generate(() -> new BigDecimal("0.00"))
                .limit(3)
                .collect(Collectors.toList());
        System.out.println(pending);
    }
}
```

```text
[0.00, 0.00, 0.00]
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

Interviewers ask you to contrast `generate` with `iterate` on dependence and ordering, explain why both are infinite, describe how `limit` short-circuits a lazy stream, and discuss thread safety of the supplier under `parallel()`. A strong answer: generate is a seedless, independent, unordered source good for IDs and random data, iterate is a seeded, dependent, ordered source good for schedules and counters, and both rely on laziness plus `limit` to stay bounded.
